==English==
{{was wotd|2006|February|27}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{qualifier|plural of anaphora}} [[anaphoras]], [[anaphors]]
* {{qualifier|plural of anaphor}} [[anaphors]]

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|grc|ἀναφορά||a carrying back}}, from {{m|grc|ἀνά||up}} + {{m|grc|φέρω||I carry}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ænəˈfɔɹə/|/ənˈæfəɹə/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-anaphora.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|anaphors|anaphora}}
{{examples|sense=rhetoric|examples=
* “Mad world! Mad kings! Mad composition!”
*: Shakespeare, ''King John'' (II i.)
}}
{{examples|sense=expression referring to a preceding expression|examples=
* That's John's car. <u>He</u> [referring to "John"] won't want to see you sitting on <u>it</u> [referring to the car].
* John had a drink. So <u>did</u> [referring to "had a drink"] Mark.
* John had been feeling rather dehydrated. Mark was even more <u>so</u> [referring to "dehydrated"].
}}

# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} The [[repetition]] of a [[phrase]] at the beginning of phrases, [[sentence]]s, or [[verse]]s, used for [[emphasis]].
#* {{RQ:Langley Rhetoric|73|'''''Anaphora''''' elegantly begins<br>With the same word or phrase successive lines.}}
#: {{ant|en|epiphora|epistrophe}}
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} An [[expression]] that can [[refer]] to virtually any referent, the specific referent being defined by context.
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} An expression that refers to a preceding expression.
#: {{hyper|en|endophora}}
#: {{coordinate terms|en|cataphora|exophora|homophora}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} The most solemn part of the [[Divine Liturgy]] or the [[Mass]] during which the [[offering]]s of bread and wine are [[consecrate]]d as body and blood of [[Christ]]

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|anaphoric}}

====Usage notes====
* In linguistics, the terms {{m|en|anaphor}} and {{m|en||anaphora}} are sometimes used interchangeably, although in some theories, a distinction is made between them. See [[w:Anaphora (linguistics)|the Wikipedia article]].

